#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;

use FindBin ();
use lib "$FindBin::Bin/../lib";

use File::Spec ();

use Encode       ();
use Getopt::Long qw(GetOptions :config no_ignore_case bundling pass_through);
use Pod::Usage   qw(pod2usage);

binmode STDOUT, ':encoding(UTF-8)';
binmode STDERR, ':encoding(UTF-8)';

# As in bin/glitchvape: decode the command line up front so a preset name or
# path containing non-ASCII is character data everywhere below.
## no critic (Variables::RequireLocalizedPunctuationVars)
@ARGV = map { Encode::decode( 'UTF-8', $_ ) } @ARGV;
## use critic

our $VERSION = '0.01';

my %opt = (
    preset => undef,
    seed   => undef,
);

GetOptions(
    'p|preset=s' => \$opt{ preset },
    's|seed=s'   => \$opt{ seed },
    'h|help'     => sub { pod2usage( -verbose => 1, -exitval => 0 ) },
    'man'        => sub { pod2usage( -verbose => 2, -exitval => 0 ) },
    'version'    => sub { print "glitchvape-gui $VERSION\n"; exit 0 },
) or pod2usage( -verbose => 0, -exitval => 2 );

# Gtk3 is loaded before GlitchVape::GUI so that a missing binding is reported
# as the one apt command that fixes it, rather than as a compile failure deep
# inside a module the user has no reason to have heard of.
my $have_gtk = eval {
    require Gtk3;
    Gtk3->import( '-init' );
    1;
};

unless ( $have_gtk )
{
    my $err = $@ || 'unknown error';
    print { *STDERR } <<"END_MISSING";
glitchvape-gui: the Gtk3 Perl binding is not available.

  sudo apt install libgtk3-perl libgtk3-imageview-perl

For an animated preview as well:

  sudo apt install gir1.2-gstreamer-1.0 gstreamer1.0-plugins-good \\
                   gstreamer1.0-gtk3 gstreamer1.0-libav

The command-line tool does not need any of these:

  glitchvape --preset vhs-decay photo.heic

($err)
END_MISSING
    exit 1;
}

my $input = shift @ARGV;

if ( defined $input && !-f $input )
{
    die "glitchvape-gui: no such file: $input\n";
}

require GlitchVape::GUI;

# Where this program is, for opening a second photograph in a window of its
# own. FindBin has already resolved it, symlinks and relative invocation
# included, and the window has no business working that out from $0 -- which
# is the launcher's fact and would be wrong the moment anything chdir'd.
my $program = File::Spec->catfile( $FindBin::RealBin, $FindBin::RealScript );

my $app = GlitchVape::GUI->new(
    input   => $input,
    preset  => $opt{ preset },
    seed    => $opt{ seed },
    program => $program,
);

exit $app->run;

__END__

=head1 NAME

glitchvape-gui - graphical front end to glitchvape

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  glitchvape-gui [options] [image]

  glitchvape-gui
  glitchvape-gui Pictures/IMG_8111.HEIC
  glitchvape-gui -p vhs-decay Pictures/IMG_8111.HEIC

 Options:
   -p, --preset NAME       preset to select at startup
   -s, --seed VALUE        seed to start from
   -h, --help              this summary
       --man               full documentation

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Presets and their parameters on the left, the render on the right. Press Apply
to render a preview; press Export to write the result at full size.

This is a front end, not a second implementation. The parameter controls are
generated from the same declarations that produce the command-line flags and
C<glitchvape --explain>, and Export calls the same C<GlitchVape::render> the
command-line tool calls -- so an exported file is reproducible from its seed
with C<glitchvape> alone.

=head2 The menu

The hamburger at the end of the header bar holds what is done rarely enough
not to want a button of its own: a new seed, the frame count and rate, saving
the current settings as a preset, C<--check-deps> in a window, emptying the
render cache, and the about box.

C<Randomize> replaced an entry field with the seed in it, because the number
was never typed: what it is only matters afterwards, for reproducing a render
that came out well, and the status line reports it after every render.

Whether to animate at all is a checkbox under Apply rather than a menu item,
since it changes what Apply does rather than how the result is shown.

C<Copy command line> puts the F<glitchvape> invocation that produces this
render on the clipboard. It is diffed against the preset rather than dumped
in full -- the state has a value for every parameter of every effect, and
printing all of them would be correct and unreadable -- so what comes out is
the handful of flags that actually differ.

=head2 The picture appears when you open it

Opening a file shows the photograph immediately, with nothing applied. There
is no pipeline to run, so it costs only the decode -- and a cache lookup the
second time. Deliberately not the preset's render even when one was named:
that can be eight seconds, and this is meant to be the thing that happens at
once. Apply is what renders the preset.

=head2 Another photograph is another window

Opening a second file starts a second copy of this program rather than
replacing what is on screen, because replacing it would throw away the
pipeline, the soundtrack and the undo history of the first one. The first Open
in a window is the exception: there is nothing to lose yet.

=head2 Undo

An undo step is one Apply. Effects are not applied on top of one another: the
pipeline sorts them by stage, because order is not a free choice -- scanlines
applied before a downsample are eaten by the resample -- so an effect enabled
later may still have to run earlier. Undo therefore restores a whole
configuration and re-renders it.

Re-rendering would be slow if it happened, so it mostly does not: every render
is cached under a digest of the settings that produced it, and a state on the
undo stack has been rendered before.

=head2 Adding a track

Ticking Animate reveals a soundtrack row. Adding a track asks for a file and
then opens a three-page wizard: crop the file on a waveform, choose the
vaporwave filters while auditioning them, and confirm. An exclamation appears
if the selection passes thirty seconds -- the loop is repeated to cover the
track, so half a minute of audio is a long encode and a large file -- but it
does not stop you.

The track appears in the preview as well as in the export, since what a
soundtrack does to a loop is exactly what a still cannot show. Removing it is
one press on the row.

C<Add generated track> beside it adds a soundtrack the program makes up. A
popover asks which kind -- phone dial tones, television static -- and the
window for that one opens already configured for it, titled with its name.

Neither knows what the kinds are: the popover and the controls both come from
L<GlitchVape::Generator>'s registry, so a new generator appears in both
without this being edited.

There is one file at most; generated tracks stack, so add as many as you like.
Everything is mixed, the file decides the length, and each generated track
covers it in whatever way suits it.

The speaker button in the toolbar plays the preview silently, for the fifth
time round a loop when the soundtrack has stopped telling you anything. It
does not touch the render: the exported file still has its sound.

The result is the same C<--audio> and C<--generate> the command-line tool
takes, so a soundtrack built here is a command line that could have been
typed.

=head2 Preview size

Previews render at a reduced size, chosen in the toolbar under the preview.
Effects work in pixels rather than fractions of the frame, so a small preview
is a fair impression of the full-size render rather than a crop of it. Export
always renders at full size.

=head2 The cache

Renders are kept in C<$XDG_CACHE_HOME/glitchvape>, or C<~/.cache/glitchvape>.
Working files go in a per-session subdirectory that is removed when the
program exits; directories left behind by a crashed session are swept at
startup; and the shared preview store is capped at 256 MB, discarding
least-recently-used entries.

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<glitchvape> for the command-line tool, L<glitchvape-batch> for many files at
once, and L<GlitchVape::GUI> for how the window is put together.

=cut
